		{
			"id": "ehviA_hxBfi43DtkX",
			"description": "Stikkbomb (Frag)",
			"local_notes": "Lore: The Ork hand grenade: a tin can packed with whatever explosive and scrap-metal shrapnel a Boy could get his hands on, thrown rather than launched, and depicted throughout Codex: Orks and Ork army background as a standard-issue Boy weapon alongside the choppa and slugga.\n\nDesign: Scaled from the Imperium Frag Grenade (3d cr ex [6d cut], data/imperium/weapons/grenades.yaml) up by one die on both the blast and the fragments for a bigger, less carefully packed charge (crude Ork munitions favour more explosive filler and scrap over precision engineering), giving 4d cr ex [7d cut]. It is not checked against the personal-armour or vehicle- AV tables, matching every other frag-grenade entry in this project: its job is infantry and light cover, not armour. Handling: thrown per Basic Set rules from the wielder's own ST and Throwing skill; 1 lb per bomb, a generic thrown-weapon weight. TL 4 reflects that a fused explosive charge in a tin can needs no real industry behind it, matching the framework's primitive- worlds TL bracket even though it is built and carried by a spacefaring greenskin empire.",
			"tags": [
				"Missile Weapon",
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"tech_level": "4",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "15",
			"base_weight": "1 lb",
			"weapons": [
				{
					"id": "WdalKqMY84DOHDHhX",
					"sv": 1,
					"damage": {
						"type": "cr ex",
						"base": "4d",
						"fragmentation": "7d",
						"fragmentation_type": "cut"
					},
					"accuracy": "0",
					"range": "x1/x1.5 x ST",
					"rate_of_fire": "1",
					"shots": "1(3)",
					"strength": "8",
					"usage": "Thrown",
					"usage_notes": "A crude pin-and-tin-can bomb; GM's discretion whether a natural 17 on the throw roll represents a genuinely early or dud fuse rather than simply a bad throw, in keeping with Ork gear's general unreliability, though this project does not assign it a formal Malf rating since it's a thrown weapon, not a mechanism that can jam.",
					"defaults": [
						{
							"type": "dx",
							"modifier": -3
						},
						{
							"type": "skill",
							"name": {
								"compare": "is",
								"qualifier": "Throwing"
							}
						}
					]
				}
			],
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eqgKH8cq5RShecrEL",
			"description": "Tankbusta Bomb",
			"local_notes": "Lore: The signature weapon of Tankbusta mobs: a heavy, shaped-charge satchel bomb thrown or slapped directly onto a vehicle's hull, magnetic clamps and all; Codex: Orks background is explicit that Tankbustas exist specifically to close with and personally wreck Imperial armour this way when rokkits alone aren't enough.\n\nDesign: Built as a hand-thrown shaped charge between the Rokkit Launcha and the Imperium Krak Missile on the anti-armour scale, sharing the same armour divisor (3) as both: 6dx3(3) cr ex (avg 63). Checked by exact dice distribution against the vehicle table: clears AV10 (99.0%) and AV11 (85.5%) reliably, drops to 36.3% at AV12 and 1.0% at AV13, failing outright at AV14 - a real threat to Chimeras, Rhinos and Sentinels, an even bet against a Predator-weight hull, and no real threat to a Leman Russ or Land Raider without a whole mob's worth of charges, matching the fiction's Tankbusta mobs swarming a vehicle rather than relying on one bomb. Against personal armour it also clears Astartes power armour 99.0% of the time and a Terminator 36.3% of the time - a genuine Marine-killer at melee range, matching its \"get close and blow it up\" doctrine. Handling: thrown per Basic Set rules; 6 lb per bomb (heavier than a stikkbomb for the real shaped-charge liner and magnetic clamp); min ST 12 is the practical minimum to throw the extra weight effectively, per Basic Set thrown- weapon convention, though a Boy or Nob manages it easily. Shots 1(10): a single-use charge with the long fuse-and-clamp setup counted as the \"reload\" between uses in a squad's shared satchel.",
			"tags": [
				"Missile Weapon",
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"tech_level": "6",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "120",
			"base_weight": "6 lb",
			"weapons": [
				{
					"id": "WHgCbnO_SLs3ot7wj",
					"sv": 1,
					"damage": {
						"type": "cr ex",
						"base": "6dx3",
						"armor_divisor": 3
					},
					"accuracy": "0",
					"range": "x1/x1.5 x ST",
					"rate_of_fire": "1",
					"shots": "1(10)",
					"strength": "12",
					"usage": "Thrown or planted",
					"usage_notes": "Planting the bomb directly against a vehicle's hull (a Committed Attack against an adjacent or grappled target, GM's discretion) removes the throwing roll's range and Acc penalties entirely - Tankbustas favour sticking a bomb to the plate over lobbing it.",
					"defaults": [
						{
							"type": "dx",
							"modifier": -3
						},
						{
							"type": "skill",
							"name": {
								"compare": "is",
								"qualifier": "Throwing"
							}
						}
					]
				}
			],
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eauFZX5uNpsycqFX3",
			"description": "Shokk Attack Gun",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Perhaps the strangest weapon in the Ork arsenal: a Mek jams a live Snotling into a chamber, opens a hole into (or through) the warp with a crude Waaagh!-powered field generator, and the Snotling is flung - still alive, and usually furious about it - directly into the enemy at the far end, materialising with enough violent, uncontrolled kinetic energy to flatten whatever it lands on or near. Codex: Orks background is explicit that the weapon is as dangerous to the Ork operating the readied hopper of Snotlings as to the enemy, since a snatched Snotling can just as easily reappear in the gun's own crew, or not reappear at all, or reappear as something other than a Snotling, and older Ork lore openly credits the whole process as barely-understood even by the Meks who build it.\n\nDesign: No framework anchor exists for this weapon and none of the framework's normal per-shot derivations (fixed Acc/RoF/Shots/Bulk figures feeding a clean to-hit-then-damage resolution) actually fit its own fiction, so this entry is built honestly as a deliberately weird weapon rather than forced into the standard mould: the \"damage\" line above is a working baseline for a Snotling's violent, uncontrolled materialisation (a solid, unarmoured-scale impact, no armour divisor - the Snotling itself does the hitting, not a penetrator; written 6dx3 cr ex with a [4d cr] bracket so the burst notation is valid) with a fragmentation-style spread bracket standing in for \"sometimes it lands short, or off to the side, or on top of something else nearby\" rather than a literal frag pattern. A GM is expected to actually run the following table on the attack roll rather than a plain hit/miss, per the fiction's own description of the gun as unpredictable in principle, not merely low-Acc in practice: on a normal hit, the Snotling appears on the target as the damage line states; on a miss, roll 1d - on 1-2 the Snotling materialises 1d yards short in a random direction and the attack still resolves against whatever (or whoever) is there instead; on 3-4 it fails to cross over at all (a wasted shot); on 5 it reappears somewhere near the gun itself instead (treat the gunner's own mob as the target); on 6 something other than the intended Snotling comes through - a chunk of terrain, a very surprised animal, or worse, at the GM's discretion, with no guaranteed damage at all. This is offered as one honest, playable version of \"warp-teleporting snotlings,\" not a claim to a single canonical mechanic; no source this project uses gives exact numbers for how the effect actually resolves, so none are invented here beyond what's needed to put the weapon on a table. Handling: 90 lb loaded (a heavy, backpack-and-hopper rig feeding live Snotlings into the chamber) with the M suffix and a raw sqrt(10x90)=30-adjacent ST 24, since it is always shown mounted on a dedicated frame or carried by an unusually large Ork rather than shouldered like a rifle. RoF 1 and Shots 1(10): one Snotling at a time, with a slow, awkward reload as the next one is wrestled into the chamber. Bulk -8 and Rcl 1 (there is no propellant charge, only the warp-field discharge) fit a heavy, backpack-scale weapon. Gunner (Cannon) stands in as the closest Basic Set skill for a heavy, mounted, indirect launcher (a snotling is a projectile, not a beam). Acc 0: by design, nobody aims a Shokk Attack Gun so much as points it and hopes. Malf 13 with its own misfire table rather than a plain malfunction result, since \"it works 'cos we fink it does\" is stretched to its breaking point by a weapon that is trying to open a hole in reality - a failure here should read as stranger and more dangerous than an ordinary jam, without this project inventing a full warp-rift subsystem the framework doesn't otherwise support.",
			"tags": [
				"Missile Weapon",
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"tech_level": "6^",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "20000",
			"base_weight": "90 lb",
			"weapons": [
				{
					"id": "WeJxtBAozZYmZbZny",
					"sv": 1,
					"damage": {
						"type": "cr ex",
						"base": "6dx3",
						"fragmentation": "4d",
						"fragmentation_type": "cr"
					},
					"accuracy": "0",
					"range": "200/600",
					"rate_of_fire": "1",
					"shots": "1(10)",
					"bulk": "-8",
					"recoil": "1",
					"strength": "24M",
					"usage": "Standard (see design)",
					"usage_notes": "Not a normal ranged weapon: see design for the full randomiser rule. Malf 13, but a malfunction here does not merely jam the gun - roll on the misfire table below instead of a normal malfunction result.",
					"defaults": [
						{
